From: crackerjacques <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
Subject: [PATCH] sprdwcn: only toggle pub_int on an actual power transition

sdio_pub_int_poweron() enables or disables the public interrupt every time it
is called, without looking at what it did last time. Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and GNSS
each bring marlin up through their own start_marlin() -> chip_power_on(), and
marlin refcounts the power rails but not this IRQ, so the second subsystem to
come up enables an interrupt that is already enabled:

  Unbalanced enable for IRQ 41
  WARNING: kernel/irq/manage.c:774 at __enable_irq+0x4c/0x7c
   enable_irq+0x6c/0xd8
   sdio_pub_int_poweron+0x28/0x44
   chip_power_on.isra.0+0x290/0x440
   marlin_set_power+0x598/0xa24
   start_marlin+0x13c/0x174
   btsprdsdio_open+0x1c/0x60 [btsprdsdio]

The state is already tracked: sdio_int.chip_power_on starts at 0 and
sdio_pub_int_register() leaves the IRQ disabled after request_irq(), so the
two agree from the outset. Swap it in and return early when nothing changed.

The threaded handler's own disable/enable pair around the callback dispatch is
self-balancing and is deliberately not routed through here.

Signed-off-by: crackerjacques <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/sprdwcn/sleep/sdio_int.c b/drivers/net/wireless/sprdwcn/sleep/sdio_int.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/sprdwcn/sleep/sdio_int.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/sprdwcn/sleep/sdio_int.c
@@ -280,7 +280,8 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL(sdio_pub_int_gnss_en0);
 
 void sdio_pub_int_poweron(bool state)
 {
-	atomic_set(&(sdio_int.chip_power_on), state);
+	if (atomic_xchg(&(sdio_int.chip_power_on), state) == state)
+		return;
 
 	if (state)
 		enable_irq(sdio_int.pub_int_num);
